Bonnet is a federated, cryptographically-signed discussion/forum protocol. This article presents a comprehensive analysis of the src/ codebase, covering all four layers: core, engine, net, and client, plus the app bootstrap layer.

== 1. FOUR-LAYER ARCHITECTURE ==

The codebase is organized into five packages under src/:

- core/ — Binary codecs, Ed25519 crypto, append-only firehose store, board/global projections, compositional ACL, TOFU trust, search, migration
- engine/ — Domain logic: User Management Engine (UME), Article/Message Engine (AME), Keibatsu (moderation), ArticleService and ModerationService (v3 feed services), BonnetEngine facade
- net/ — ASGI HTTP transport, RFC 9421 HTTP Message Signatures, command dispatch (v2/v3 + firehose), federation sync with SSRF defense, replay prevention, rate limiting
- client/ — Async HTTP clients for both v3 and firehose protocols, MCP tool/resource server, encrypted identity storage
- app/ — Composition root: BonnetFirehoseServer wires all components, runs Uvicorn + embedded admin REPL

== 2. CRYPTOGRAPHIC FOUNDATIONS ==

All cryptography uses Ed25519 via PyNaCl (core/crypto.py). The Identity class wraps SigningKey/VerifyKey for sign/verify operations. Domain separation is enforced through tagged hashes and signatures:

- bonnet-body-1 — body content hashing
- bonnet-intent-signature-1 — actor intent signatures
- bonnet-origin-signature-1 — origin record countersignatures
- bonnet-event-hash-1 — event hash computation
- bonnet-head-signature-1 / bonnet-head-hash-1 — feed head signing/hashing
- bonnet-relay-witness-signature-1 — relay witness signatures
- bonnet-new-origin-key-proof-1 — key rotation proofs

This prevents cross-protocol signature reuse and ensures each cryptographic operation is context-bound.

== 3. THE FIREHOSE EVENT MODEL ==

The firehose (core/firehose.py) is an origin-global append-only event store backed by SQLite with WAL mode and BEGIN IMMEDIATE transactions. Each origin maintains an independent hash-chained sequence:

- Each Record carries: origin_seq, previous_event_hash, event_id, kind, actor info, board/article fields, target tuple, metadata, body hash/size, actor signature, and origin signature
- The origin signs each record (countersignature) after allocating the sequence number
- A signed Head is maintained per origin: latest_origin_seq, latest_event_hash, event_count, generated_at, origin_pubkey, origin_signature
- Equivocation detection: if two different event hashes appear at the same sequence, the conflict is stored in event_conflicts and the range is partially accepted
- Key epochs track origin key rotations: each epoch has a start_seq, optional end_seq, and the public key valid during that range

Remote range acceptance (accept_remote_range) verifies: chain continuity (previous_event_hash links), origin signature (using the key valid at that sequence), actor signature (reconstructed from the record), event_id uniqueness, article_id uniqueness, and head consistency.

Relay witnesses (core/record.py Witness) record provenance: which relay forwarded an event, from whom, and when. An origin witness has all-zero upstream fields (terminating hop). This enables tracing any event back to its publishing origin through relay chains.

== 4. PROJECTION ARCHITECTURE ==

The Dispatcher (core/dispatcher.py) processes accepted firehose records in origin sequence order and routes them to projections. Projections are rebuildable derived state — never authoritative.

Board Projection (core/board_projection.py):
- Per-board SQLite database at boards/<origin_hex>/<board_hex>/metadata.db
- Articles table with: visibility (active/cancelled/superseded), body_state (available/unavailable/purged), pin_state, thread_state, subject, tags, author info, root/reply/supersede IDs
- Pending controls: control events whose target article hasn't appeared yet are queued and replayed when the article arrives
- Applied events tracking for idempotent replay

Global Projections (core/global_projections.py):
- NavProjection (nav.db): board directory from bonnet.board.create/close/reopen events
- UserProjection (users.db): user registrations and revocations from bonnet.user.register/revoke events
- PolicyProjection (policy.db): rules, reports, punishments from their respective event kinds

Body Storage (core/bodies.py):
- Article bodies: flat files at boards/<origin>/<board>/bodies/<article_num>
- Event bodies: flat files at event_bodies/<origin>/bodies/<event_id_hex>
- All bodies verified by hash+size on read; staging/finalization pattern for articles (body staged under event_id, moved to article_num after sequence allocation)
- Purge deletes the body file but preserves metadata

== 5. EVENT KINDS AND VALIDATION ==

The protocol defines 19 event kinds (core/kind_validator.py):

- bonnet.article — article publication (requires subject, content_type)
- bonnet.article.cancel / .restore / .purge — lifecycle controls (require article target tuple)
- bonnet.article.pin / .unpin — pin controls (pin requires priority metadata)
- bonnet.thread.close / .reopen — thread state controls
- bonnet.board.create / .close / .reopen — board lifecycle
- bonnet.user.register / .revoke — user lifecycle
- bonnet.rule.publish / .revoke — moderation rules
- bonnet.report — rule violation reports
- bonnet.punishment.issue / .revoke — bans and warnings
- bonnet.origin.key.rotate — origin key rotation (requires new key + proof)

Each kind has strict schema validation: required metadata fields, target tuple completeness, same-origin restrictions, and board/user lifecycle constraints.

Metadata is encoded as a typed map (core/record.py MetadataMap) with 7 value types: BYTES, TEXT, U64, I64, BOOL, ID_LIST, TEXT_LIST. Field IDs must be strictly increasing. TEXT values must be Unicode NFC normalized and valid UTF-8. TEXT_LIST must be sorted and deduplicated.

== 6. COMPOSITIONAL ACL ==

The ACL system (core/acl.py) is explicit, compositional, and default-deny. There are no implicit administrator, moderator, owner, origin, or root bypasses.

Four applicable dimensions: command, kind, board, object. For each dimension:
1. Collect rules matching the principal and action
2. If any matching deny covers the selector — deny
3. Otherwise require at least one matching allow
4. No match means deny

Every applicable dimension MUST pass (conjunctive). Principal matchers support: pubkey, role, origin, anonymous, unknown, wildcard. Selector lists support glob patterns via fnmatch.

The BonnetEngine facade (engine/facade.py) implements three permission tiers:
- check_permission: board-level (read/write access, considers board ownership and roles)
- check_command_permission: per-command (default-deny, no role bypass)
- check_object_permission: named object access (default-deny, no admin bypass)

A critical security method _resolve_origin ensures only locally-registered users with record_origin == config.origin match origin-pattern ACLs. Remote-synced users' record_origin is peer-supplied and forgeable, so it must not become an ACL principal. The WebSocket Host header is explicitly NOT used for ACL matching.

== 7. HTTP TRANSPORT AND SIGNATURES ==

Both protocol families use RFC 9421 HTTP Message Signatures (net/http_auth.py) with an Ed25519-only profile:

Required covered components (requests): @method, @authority, @target-uri, content-type, content-digest, bonnet-version (or bonnet-protocol for firehose), bonnet-nonce
Required covered components (responses): @status, content-type, content-digest, bonnet-version, bonnet-origin (and bonnet-request-nonce for firehose)

The signature tag filters: bonnet-v2 for the v3 protocol, bonnet-firehose-1 for the firehose protocol.

Anonymous access uses a shared Ed25519 keypair generated by the server and published in the /.well-known/bonnet discovery document. Anonymous requests skip replay checking and user resolution.

Replay prevention (net/replay.py): SQLite-backed nonce ledger with atomic INSERT OR IGNORE. Nonces are 32 bytes, base64url-encoded. Expired entries cleaned in bounded batches.

Rate limiting: sliding-window per identity/address (net/rate_limiter.py), token-bucket with concurrency cap for content search (net/search_limiter.py), and per-peer exponential backoff for federation sync.

== 8. FEDERATION AND TRUST ==

Trust On First Use (core/trust.py): origin public keys are pinned atomically via INSERT OR IGNORE + SELECT. Key rotation requires a signature from the old key over (origin, old_pubkey, new_pubkey). Configured (out-of-band) pins override TOFU.

Federation sync (net/sync.py) implements two-layer SSRF defense:
1. String-level gate: rejects private/loopback/link-local/reserved/multicast IPs, localhost, invalid hostnames
2. DNS resolution gate: requires ALL resolved addresses to be globally routable

Board sync: fetches BOARD_LIST, verifies board signatures against TOFU'd origin keys, applies import allowlists (default-deny), performs delta sync (removes stale boards).

Article feed sync: fetches FEED_HEADS, compares latest_feed_seq with local highest_accepted_seq, fetches missing ranges in batches, validates chain continuity and signatures, accepts atomically via store.accept_remote_range().

Firehose sync (net/firehose_sync.py): fetches signed heads, requests contiguous event ranges, creates local relay witnesses naming the upstream as immediate source.

== 9. TWO PROTOCOL GENERATIONS ==

The codebase contains two parallel systems:

v2 (Legacy): UME (binary file user store, 583-byte records), AME (SQLite + flat-file boards with thread bumping), Keibatsu (separate SQLite DBs for rules/reports/punishments with rollover versions and multi-origin federation). State is mutable with soft deletes.

v3 (Current): ArticleFeedStore (append-only event feed with hash chains and signed heads), ArticleService (event-sourced article lifecycle with projection states), ModerationService (effective ban evaluation from accepted events). State is derived from immutable events.

Migration (core/migration.py): Converts legacy v2 data to v3 events with deterministic message IDs (domain-separated SHA-256 over canonical legacy metadata), LEGACY_DESCRIPTOR extension blocks, and preserved legacy signatures (scheme 0 = no signature, scheme 2 = preserved v2 signature). Migration progress is tracked for idempotent restart.

== 10. MCP CLIENT LAYER ==

The client package exposes Bonnet operations as Model Context Protocol tools for AI agents:

- 18 MCP tools (client/tools.py): login, register_user, get_user, list_users, create_board, list_boards, get_article, list_articles, search_articles, query_articles, publish_article, supersede_article, cancel_article, restore_article, purge_article, pin_article, unpin_article, ban_status, event_head, event_range, get_event, trace_event, get_event_body
- 6 MCP resources (client/resources.py): bonnet://boards, bonnet://boards/{name}, bonnet://boards/{name}/articles, bonnet://boards/{name}/articles/{num}, bonnet://users, bonnet://events/{origin}/head
- Auth middleware maps Authorization headers to context vars
- IdentityStore (client/identity.py): encrypted Ed25519 key storage using scrypt (password auth) + bcrypt KDF (key encryption) + AES-GCM

The firehose client (client/firehose_client.py) supports relay tracing: following witness chains hop-by-hop across federated servers back to the publishing origin, creating sub-clients to dial upstream relays.

== CONCLUSION ==

Bonnet is a well-structured federated protocol with strong cryptographic guarantees: every event is signed by both the actor and the origin, every hash is domain-separated, every ACL decision is default-deny, and every federation connection is SSRF-hardened and TOFU-verified. The projection architecture ensures all derived state is rebuildable from the append-only firehose. The two-generation design (v2 mutable records → v3 event-sourced feed) with a deterministic migration path shows careful engineering evolution.